  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1990s/252-blockhouse-and-riverview.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Closing Out the 21st Century page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In August, members of Dakota Stage Ltd. opened their seventh annual performance of the Walking Drama at Fort Abraham Lincoln. Key characters included {% person "elizabeth-bacon-custer", "Elizabeth Bacon Custer" %}Libbie Custer{% endperson %} and troopers of the Seventh Cavalry. A Northern Lights Chorus ensemble provided music before the performance. Cast and audience members walked along the bluffs overlooking the Missouri River, near the place where friends and families of soldiers killed at the Battle of the Little Bighorn had awaited news of the encounter.</p>

<h3>1993</h3>

<p>In December, the Mandan School Board purchased the outdoor swimming-pool property adjacent to Custer Elementary School from the Mandan Park Board for $1. The pool had closed permanently after the 1991 season because of high maintenance and repair costs. The School Board planned to convert the property into a parking lot or playground. Demolition and filling of the pool, estimated to cost $45,000, were completed in 1996.</p>

<h3>1994</h3>

<h3>1995</h3>

<h3>1996</h3>

<p>In April, a groundbreaking ceremony marked the start of Main Street reconstruction. The initial demolition uncovered and removed remnants of the trolley tracks that had connected downtown with the city's southwest corner. That part of town, known as "The Syndicate," included the former Missouri Valley Mill and Elevator along the Heart River, now the site of the Mandan City Shops and baseball and softball complex.</p>

<h3>1997</h3>

<h3>1999</h3>

<p>The Davenport Hill Bike Trail opened between the Mandan Community Center and the interstate in northwest Mandan. The 6.3-mile trail was a joint effort by the Mandan Parks and Recreation Department, Dakota Roughrider Club, and Dakota Cyclery staff. It hosted the State Mountain Biking Championship in October.</p>

<p>In November, a T-shaped groundwater oil-recovery trench was completed south of Main Street in the 200 block west. It had 11 recovery sumps and belt skimmers housed in structures the city engineer repeatedly called "outhouses." BNSF contractor ThermoRETEC designed and operated the system, which could recover as much as 500 gallons of oil per day.</p>
